{
  "summary": "One finding needs your attention soon: a scheduled task named \\TelemetryRefresh downloads text from the internet and runs it as commands, which is a pattern malware often uses. The other finding is a routine check that you recognize one of the administrator accounts on this computer. Look at the scheduled task first, then confirm the account is one you know.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Scheduled task that downloads and runs commands from the internet",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:2041796b"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task called \\TelemetryRefresh is set up to run automatically under your account (<user>). It is currently in the Ready state. Its command fetches a file from a web address and passes whatever it receives straight to the Windows command prompt to run. Whoever controls that web address can change what runs on your computer at any time, without you seeing it. Legitimate software rarely works this way, which is why the rule rated it high. The task runs with limited rights, not as an administrator, which reduces the harm somewhat but does not make it safe. If you didn't create this task yourself or can't tie it to a program you trust, treat it as a serious warning sign. The report shows the recommended fix for this finding."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize an administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> is a local account with full administrator rights. Having a separate administrator account is often normal and even good practice, so this is rated low. It's worth a quick check that you know who created this account and why, because an unfamiliar administrator account can be a sign of a break-in. Given the suspicious scheduled task above, it's especially worth taking a moment to confirm this one is expected. The report shows what to do if you don't recognize it."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
